==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[consultation]] ''(Only noun form in UK)''

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|consulter|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|consultare||to deliberate, consult|lang=la}}, frequentative of {{term|consulere||to consult, deliberate, consider, reflect upon, ask advice}}, from {{term|com-||together}} + {{term|-sulere}}, of uncertain origin.

===Pronunciation===
; Noun
* {{enPR|kŏn'sŭlt ''or'' kənsŭlt'}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈkɒnsʌlt/|/kənˈsʌlt/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈkɑnsʌlt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-consult-noun.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
; Verb
* {{enPR|kənsŭlt'}}, {{IPA|/kənˈsʌlt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-consult-verb.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}}: The act of consulting or deliberating; consultation; also, the result of consultation; determination; decision.
#: ''The council broke; And all grave '''consults''' dissolved in smoke.'' -[[w:John Dryden|John Dryden]].
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}}: A council; a meeting for consultation.
#: ''A '''consult''' of coquettes.'' -[[w:Jonathan Swift|Jonathan Swift]].
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}}: Agreement; concert.
# {{context|US|lang=en}}: A visit, e.g. to a doctor; a [[consultation]].

====Usage notes====
* The noun [[consult]] is avoided in British English, favoring [[consultation]] instead. In AmE, they are merely synonyms.

====Synonyms====
* [[consultation]]

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To seek the opinion or advice of another; to take counsel; to deliberate together; to confer.
#: ''Let us '''consult''' upon to-morrow's business.'' -[[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]]
#: ''All the laws of England have been made by the kings of England, '''consulting''' with the nobility and commons.'' - [[w:Thomas Hobbes|Thomas Hobbes]].
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[advise]] or offer expertise.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To work as a [[consultant]] or [[contractor]] rather than as a full-time [[employee]] of a firm.
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To ask advice of; to seek the opinion of; to apply to for information or instruction; to refer to; as, to consult a physician; to consult a dictionary.
#: ''Men forgot, or feared, to '''consult''' ... ; they were content to '''consult''' libraries.'' - [[w:William Whewell|William Whewell]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To have reference to, in judging or acting; to have regard to; to consider; as, to consult one's wishes.
#: ''We are ... to '''consult''' the necessities of life, rather than matters of ornament and delight.'' -L'Estrange.
# {{context|transitive|obsolete|lang=en}}: To deliberate upon; to take for.
#: ''Many things were there '''consulted''' for the future, yet nothing was positively resolved.'' -[[w:Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon|Edward Hyde Clarendon]].
# {{context|transitive|obsolete|lang=en}}: To bring about by counsel or contrivance; to devise; to contrive.
#: ''Thou hast '''consulted''' shame to thy use by cutting off many people.'' - Bible, Heb. ii. 10.

